        },
        "text_amE9ti": {
          "type": "text",
          "settings": {
            "text": "<p>If you've ever had a professional lymphatic drainage facial, you know every therapist starts at the <strong>neck first</strong> — and for good reason.</p><p>They understand that <strong>80% of the results come from the neck, and 20% from the face.</strong> You can get most of the de-puffing benefit just by massaging the neck, opening the lymph nodes, and letting gravity drain the fluid.</p><p>Here's the part the beauty industry doesn't advertise: the highest density of lymph nodes in your body sits in your neck. That's a well-established fact in medicine.</p>",
            "text_font": "body",
            "text_size": "subtext-md",
            "text_max_size": 32,
            "text_line_height": 1.6,
            "text_letter_spacing": 0,
            "text_capitalize": false,
            "secondary_color": false,
            "highlighted_text": "none",
            "highlighted_scribble": "circle"
          }
        },
        "text_JEDzr3": {
          "type": "text",
          "settings": {
            "text": "<p>The cervical (neck) region contains the largest concentration of lymph nodes in the human body — the main gateway for fluid leaving the face</p>",
